> Shell Scripts

There are a number of helper shell script that can be used for various tasks, with the main shell scripts being found in the Cerberus source directory (src).

*NOTE:* You will more than likely require either elevated privileges, or set the script execution policy ([[../../src/builder.ps1|see the actual Powershell script for information]]). 

>> The Builder & Gen Links Tool Scripts

There are three scripts within the [[../../src|Cerberus/src]] directory. These are use to actually build Cerberus and create symbolic links to various tools that are used by Cerberus.

>> The builder.sh & builder.ps1 scripts

These are scripts to build Cerberus.~n
Instructions for use: [[../../src/docs/BUILDING_WINNT.txt|Windows]], 
[[../../src/docs/BUILDING_MACOS.txt|macOS]], 
[[../../src/docs/BUILDING_LINUX.txt|Linux]]

>> The gen-tool-links.sh & gen-tool-links.ps1

These two scripts are used to generate symbolic links in the Cerberus directory to external tools.~n
To see the options available, type the command and pass the option -h.

>> The gen-mingw-dll-imports.ps1 script

If MinGW installed. The [[../../tools/gen-mingw-dll-imports.ps1|gen-mingw-dll-imports.ps1]] script is used to generate import libraries from shared libraries created with Visual Studio.

*NOTE:* MinGW and Visual Studio generate different export symbol names for C++, and are therefore the tools that come with MinGW to do the conversion will not work.
Only shared libraries that export C style naming symbols will work.

>> The build_libs.sh & build_libs.ps1 scripts

These two scripts are found in the [[../../modules_ext/cmake_demo|modules_ext/cmake_demo/cmake_demo]]. They are used to build and manage the demonstration libraries for use with the [[../../examples/cmake|CMake target examples]].~n
To see the options, type the command and pass the -h option.